course: dictionary
lesson: 7824
title: Knots that keep records
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A quipu is a word from the Inca people of South America. Use the context to work out its meaning: «They had no writing as we know it, but kept records with knotted strings called quipu.» The words called quipu tell you that the knotted strings are the quipu.
explain: The second sentence gives more clues: «The Inca kept records with quipu, strings with knots of different colours and positions, to count people, taxes and goods.» Records are information kept for later, and count tells you the strings were used for numbers.
explain: Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try «knotted strings» in the sentence instead of quipu and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A quipu is a set of knotted coloured strings. It is a noun, and the plural is quipus.
fact: «Quipu» means knotted strings the Inca used to record numbers and information.
fact: The Inca did not use writing as we know it, so quipu were an important way to keep records.
fact: The type and position of each knot could stand for units, tens and hundreds, which is a base-10 system.
fact: Trained officials called quipucamayocs read the quipu.
exercise: «They had no writing as we know it, but kept records with knotted strings called quipu.» What is a quipu? | *A set of knotted coloured strings used to record information | A large stone temple | A type of Inca hat | A kind of Inca pottery
exercise: The Inca used the colours of the strings to show something. What did the colours stand for? | The weather | The names of the rivers | The number of days in a year | *Different things being counted
exercise: Find and copy one word from «The Inca kept records with quipu, strings with knots of different colours and positions, to count people, taxes and goods.» that means to add up numbers. | record | goods | strings | *count
test: In a quipu, the knots use a base-10 system. Which position stands for the biggest number? | Units | *Hundreds | Tens | Strings
test: What were the trained officials who read quipu called? | Scribes | Quipus | *Quipucamayocs | Incas
exam: How could the Inca keep records without writing? | They used the Roman alphabet on paper | They carved their numbers into stone walls | *They used knotted strings called quipu to record numbers and information | They drew pictures of every person on clay tablets
